Fix the timer interrupt number in the QEMU virt BSP.

The BSP declares the system tick on IRQ 1 with a comment copied from an
unrelated board. IRQ 1 is a software-generated interrupt; the real source is
the ARM generic virtual timer, GIC PPI 11 = IRQ 27 - which is what the timer
callouts actually program (mrrc/mcrr p15, 3, ..., c14 = CNTV_CVAL).

Without this the tick never fires: clock_gettime() returns zero forever,
sleep() never returns, and Go aborts with "nanotime returning zero", while
ClockCycles() keeps counting and hides the problem.

--- a/src/hardware/startup/boards/virt/init_qtime_virt.c
+++ b/src/hardware/startup/boards/virt/init_qtime_virt.c
@@
-	qtime->intr = 1;	/* GPT1 irq */
+	qtime->intr = 27;	/* GIC PPI 11 = ARM generic VIRTUAL timer on QEMU virt */
